{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbqZWpMfyFbRs9XYBWCC", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7454, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7454}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "34f804a7-21a7-4232-ba01-948bd3739684", "uuid": "e3486d31-6d76-4860-b3ec-b2ac92922809", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T16:32:26.890Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbqZWTnKPKu4AyixUFHg"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbqZWpMfyFbRs9XYBWCC", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Banker's rounding** (also called round-half-to-even, or ROUND_HALF_EVEN) works like ordinary rounding except when a value is exactly halfway between two candidates. In that case it rounds to the nearest **even** digit.\n\n| Value | Round half up | Banker's |\n|-------|---------------|----------|\n| 2.5 \u2192 integer | 3 | **2** |\n| 3.5 \u2192 integer | 4 | **4** |\n| 0.125 \u2192 2 dp | 0.13 | **0.12** |\n| 0.135 \u2192 2 dp | 0.14 | **0.14** |\n\nAnything that isn't exactly halfway rounds normally.\n\n**Why accountants like it**\n\n- **It avoids systematic bias.** Round-half-up always pushes ties upward. Across thousands of line items, that adds a small but consistent upward drift. Half-even rounds ties up about half the time and down about half the time, so the errors tend to cancel.\n- **Totals stay closer to the true sum.** Over large datasets, such as interest calculations, tax per line, or batch settlements, the total rounding error stays near zero instead of growing.\n- **It's fair to both parties.** Neither the buyer nor the seller is consistently favored, which matters for audits and disputes.\n- **It's a standard.** It's the default in IEEE 754 floating point and in many financial and regulatory contexts, so results are reproducible and easy to defend.\n\n**Caveats**\n\n- The benefit only shows up with many tie cases. This is common with values like x.5 or x.xx5, but not with random data that rarely lands exactly on a tie.\n- Binary floats rarely represent decimal ties exactly. In practice you need decimal types (for example, Python's `decimal` with `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`) to get the intended behavior.\n- Some jurisdictions or contracts mandate round-half-up, so check the applicable rule.\n\nThis project's stored convention follows this approach. Tax amounts use `decimal.ROUND_HALF_EVEN`, rounded per line item and then summed, with no rounding of the invoice total."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7454, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7454}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "34f804a7-21a7-4232-ba01-948bd3739684", "uuid": "caaf67e0-c4f4-44e2-a505-b6fd52c39ca7", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T16:32:31.734Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbqZWTnKPKu4AyixUFHg"}
